There are a lot of cases where a difference in degree can lead to
a difference in kind. A star is wildly different from a hydrogen/helium 
mixture in a tank.

>>Still, if you are thinking
>>stupidly or just inefficiently, speeding up that process will probably just
>>yield more stupid and inefficient thinking.

That may depend on what's causing the stupidity or inefficiency.

For example, I can think of "stupidity" being caused by either too
good or too poor of a memory. If the memory is too good, then the
first half-baked idea gets fixed in concrete and can't be modified
by either new thoughts or new input. If the memory is too poor, then
the beginning of the thought is forgotten as one tries to generate
the middle and/or the rest of one's knowledge can't be integrated
into the new idea. 

This adds up to two kinds of stupidity--stubborness and confusion. 

For that matter, associational ability might need to be in an
optimal middle range--too little, and the rest of one's knowledge
doesn't get involved in the new idea (either to check it or to
expand it)--too much, and there's such a flood of association
that there's no way to check on whether any of it makes sense.
(The latter sounds like florid schizophrenia.) 

The optima wouldn't be single ranges for everyone--for example,
I think that someone with a better memory could handle more
association.

Also, there are some other essential abilities that I don't
have names for--the ability to check one idea against another
and the ability to keep one's memories organized.

I doubt that there's any way to speed up the human brain/mind
equally in all its aspects, especially if you're trying to make
it go as fast as possible. (Lebovitz's Third Law*--nothing
scales up or down entirely equally.)  

