Vince Kerchner wrote:
> However, this is an often glossed-over issue concerning uploading:
> the "blind" copying of neural architecture without regard for design
> or understanding.  While we may end up with faster perception and
> cognition, we'll also end up with faster neurosis, epilepsy, depression,
> schizophrenia, and a host of other physiological and psychological
> disorders.  I'd much rather know enough about how the brain and mind
> work to leave all that behind.

"Blind uploading" (handy term! let's put it in the lexicon) can make one 
improvement right away: just avoid copying the behavior of the brain during 
sugar-crash, fatigue, serotonin deficiency and the like.  It might even be 
feasible to isolate the effects of your favorite drugs on various _parts_ 
of your brain, and control them with handy switches.

Anders Sandberg replied to Vince:
> This is important (as discussed in the upgrading thread). We must find 
> both ways to encode the human brain more efficiently in a computer (do
> we really need to store the electric data of each neuron, or can they
> be chunked to functional units?), and to prepare ourselves psychologically
> before uploading. Of course, uploaded brains may be easier to cure from
> lots of problems ( . . . ). 

Surgery to control epilepsy will be more easily reversible!

Logical chunking is an interesting problem.  Are there clusters of neurons 
such that each member's output function is smooth and monotone, but the 
cluster's output function is chaotic?

The most worrisome problem I see, though, is learning.  It's fairly simple, 
in concept, to make replacement neurons that mimic your brain's function at 
this moment; much harder to get them to make new connections.  (Platt's THE 
SILICON MAN mentioned this problem obliquely, saying that an uploaded child 
will never grow up.)

