>>frame is irrelevant. If we can solve problems in one time frame
>>we can solve them in another, Scientists do it every day.
>>
>>       >and would go  insane if the world (from our perspective)
>>       >slowed down by a factor of  millions.
>>
>>Hey, speak for yourself. There will always be fast changing
>>things both natural and , no doubt, artificial at the molecular
>>level to keep us busy. Then there's 3-D space, 10 billion
>>telescopes pointed in different directions would not be boring
>>and don't forget other uploads and ...
>>
>Good point--I think that people are optimized for a lot of
>input (isolation tanks are fun and educational (for some
>people) for a few hours, and torture for days). Just how
>much presumably varies--some people can feel that they're
>getting adequate input from a mere computer screen for long
>periods, and others don't.

Can you imagine staring at a computer screen for a hundred years straight? 
 I think Jordan has a point here.  Suppose the first upload were able to 
think a billion times faster than ordinary humans.  With each passing 
second, the equivalent of 32 years would pass from the upload's point of 
view.  Meaningful discourse with humans would be tedious at best - say hi 
and wait 32 years for a reply (rather like interstellar radio 
communications).  If the second upload "went on-line" the following day, two 
_million_ years or more could have passed for upload #1.  Somehow I doubt 
that any amount of reading material would be adequate for such a wait.  And 
without direct access to a chemistry/nanotech lab or  telescopes, one could 
only _think_ about molecules and stars without ever _interacting_ with them.

This reminds me of Steven King's _The Jaunt_, wherein people who travel 
instantaneously though hyperspace must be drugged up so they don't think 
during the journey.  Hyperspace, it seems, distorts perception of time in 
unknown ways, and all attempts to travel while conscious have proven fatal 
to the traveller.  A boy decides that he wants to see what hyperspace is 
like, so he holds his breath while the gas-drug is administered.  Upon 
arrival his parents are horrified to see the boy, his hair turned 
snow-white, gouge out his eyes screaming, "Forever's a mighty long time!"

How can we produce a community of uploads if the first ones tend to go nutty 
on us?

We could try to have several uploads go on-line at once.  But what if they 
don't get along?  Is a million years enough time to make bitter enemies into 
best friends?

One solution to these potential problems would be, as Jordan points out, to 
create a VR with which the upload can interact.  In the Cyberpunk RPG, 
travellers on multi-year interplanetary voyages a put into a state called 
"braindance" in which the ship's computer provides the passengers with an 
interactive VR.  This is done to prevent the sort of mental aberrations 
which tend to develop during long periods of confinement.

Similarly, although there are good reasons why we'd want to hook the first 
uploads up to telescopes and nanofactories, there are also good reasons why 
we _wouldn't_ want such connections (especially if early uploads tend to go 
crazy).  I think a more practical alternative would be to provide the 
uploads with very convincing VR environments so that they may perform 
(virtual) experiments with nanomachines, etc. instead of just thinking about 
them.

Maybe we'd even want to let the uploads be "brains in vats" for a while, to 
make sure they're working properly before unleashing them into the physical 
world.

